      <!-- Card Title -->
      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl md:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>Why Metadata Removal Matters</span>
      </h2>

      <!-- Story Narrative -->
      <div class="space-y-3.5 text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-gray-200">
          Imagine taking a photo of a morning coffee on your apartment balcony and sharing it online. To your friends, it’s just a cozy snapshot. But embedded deep inside the image file is a silent telemetry beacon: the exact latitude and longitude coordinates pointing to your building, the altitude indicating your floor number, your smartphone's unique factory serial number, and a timestamp accurate to the millisecond.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-300">
          Without metadata removal, anyone downloading the file — from automated web crawlers to malicious actors — can cross-reference those coordinates with public street view databases. Over time, recurring photos posted across social forums allow strangers to map your home location, daily routine, and device identity without your knowledge.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-300">
          <strong>CleanPhoto</strong> solves this by acting as your local privacy airlock. Before your image ever touches an upload input, our in-memory engine draws the visual pixels onto an offscreen canvas and re-encodes the file in your browser's RAM. All GPS coordinates, device fingerprints, and serial tags are destroyed — ensuring you share only the picture, never your location.
        </p>
      </div>

